Step 1 — Check the files

First, run:

ls

You will see:

data.txt

This tells us that the file containing the password is data.txt.

Step 2 — Read the file

Run:

cat data.txt

You will see something similar to:

Gur cnffjbeq vf ...

This doesn't look like normal English.

The important clue is that the challenge uses ROT13.

Step 3 — Understand ROT13

ROT13 means Rotate by 13 positions.

For example:

A → N
B → O
C → P
...
M → Z

And the second half goes back:

N → A
O → B
P → C
...
Z → M

So:

Gur → The
cnffjbeq → password

You don't need to manually decode every character.

Step 4 — Use tr to decode ROT13

Run:

cat data.txt | tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'
What happens here?

The command has two parts.

Part 1:
cat data.txt

Reads the contents of data.txt.

Part 2:
tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'

tr translates characters.

It changes:

A → N
B → O
C → P
...

This performs the ROT13 conversion.

Step 5 — Understand the pipe |

This symbol:

|

is called a pipe.

It takes the output from the command on the left and sends it as input to the command on the right.

So:

cat data.txt | tr ...

means:

Read data.txt → send the text to tr → convert the ROT13 characters.

Step 6 — Get the password

After running:

cat data.txt | tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'

you will get:

The password is XXXXXXXX

The text after "The password is" is the password for Bandit Level 12.

Step 7 — Connect to Level 12

If you're currently logged in as bandit11, you can exit:

exit

Then connect:

ssh bandit12@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

Enter the password you decoded in Step 6.

